      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Sonnet Vorthos cast Hordewing Skaab (5 mana) before playing the Island from hand. With 6 lands on board and needing 5 for the Skaab, they could have played Island first (going to 7 lands) and then cast the Skaab, leaving 2 mana open instead of 1. The extra mana could be relevant for holding up interaction or using Eloise's clue tokens. Additionally, casting Arcane Signet (2 mana) first and then playing Island would leave them with 6 mana available for future turns' bigger plays like Eater of Hope (7 mana). The sequencing of land drop before spell is a basic principle that was violated here.",
      "action_taken": "Cast Hordewing Skaab without first playing the Island from hand, leaving only 1 mana open.",
      "better_line": "Play Island first, then cast Arcane Signet (2 mana), leaving 4 mana open and ramping for next turn to enable Eater of Hope. Alternatively, play Island then cast Hordewing Skaab to leave 2 mana open.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 134,
      "player": "Sonnet Vorthos",
      "decision_index": 116,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Hordewing Skaab is a 3/3 flyer that could attack Gem3F Stoic or Qwen3 Spike with no viable blockers (neither has flyers or reach). This is free 3 damage plus Hordewing Skaab's triggered ability would allow drawing and discarding a card if it deals combat damage, providing valuable card filtering. Leaving it back gains nothing since there's no need to hold it on defense.",
      "action_taken": "Attacked with only Eloise, leaving Hordewing Skaab back.",
      "better_line": "Attack with both Eloise and Hordewing Skaab, directing the Skaab at an opponent with no flying blockers (Stoic or Spike) for free damage and card filtering.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 136,
      "player": "Sonnet Vorthos",
      "decision_index": 116,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Attacking into Gem3P Coach when Serra Angel (4/4 flying, vigilance) can block one of the flying attackers, potentially trading with or killing Hordewing Skaab or Eloise. Gem3F Stoic has no flying blockers, meaning both Eloise (4/4) and Hordewing Skaab (3/3) would deal 7 unblocked damage with zero risk.",
      "action_taken": "Chose to attack Gem3P Coach who has Serra Angel as a flying blocker",
      "better_line": "Attack Gem3F Stoic for 7 unblocked flying damage with no risk of losing creatures to blocks",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 136,
      "player": "Sonnet Vorthos",
      "decision_index": 116,
    },
    {
      "severity": "questionable",
      "description": "Gem3F Stoic is attacking Sonnet Vorthos who has Hordewing Skaab (3/3 flyer) on the board. Sram (2/2) and Puresteel Paladin (2/2) are ground creatures and cannot be blocked by the flying Skaab, so they'll get through for damage either way. However, Qwen3 Spike at 31 life has zero creatures and no blockers, making them a completely open target. Attacking Sonnet Vorthos at 28 life versus Qwen3 Spike at 31 life is close, but the reasoning about 'applying pressure to the one with the board state' is backwards — you typically want to pressure the player who is weak and open, not the one who has blockers and a developed board. Sonnet Vorthos has a zombie-themed graveyard engine and attacking them could be strategically sound to keep them low, but Qwen3 Spike with 7 cards in hand and ramping could become a bigger threat. This is debatable multiplayer politics.",
      "action_taken": "Attacked Sonnet Vorthos with ground creatures (Sram and Puresteel Paladin are 2/2s that can't be blocked by the flying Hordewing Skaab anyway)",
      "better_line": "Attacking Qwen3 Spike who has no creatures and no blockers is the safer and more efficient choice for guaranteed damage, though attacking Vorthos is defensible since the ground creatures get through regardless of the flyer",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 141,
      "player": "Gem3F Stoic",
      "decision_index": 127,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Passed the turn with 6+ mana available and Imprisoned in the Moon in hand, while Kaalia of the Vast (a high-priority threat that cheats creatures into play) is on the battlefield. No mana was used this postcombat main phase.",
      "action_taken": "Passed without casting anything, leaving all mana unused.",
      "better_line": "Cast Imprisoned in the Moon on Kaalia of the Vast to neutralize her before she can generate more value. Alternatively, if the Unknown option was Galea, casting the commander would also be a reasonable use of mana.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 151,
      "player": "Gem3F Stoic",
      "decision_index": 134,
    },
    {
      "severity": "questionable",
      "description": "Attacking Qwen3 Spike (31 life) instead of Sonnet Vorthos (24 life) with Kaalia of the Vast is debatable. Sonnet Vorthos is at lower life (24 vs 31) and has a more dangerous engine potential with Wilhelt in the command zone and graveyard cards like Midnight Reaper, Lord of the Accursed, and Eloise. However, Sonnet has Hordewing Skaab (3/3 flying) which could block Kaalia (2/2 flying), killing her. The reasoning about Kaalia's safety is valid - attacking into a 3/3 flyer with a 2/2 flyer would be suicidal without a combat trick. That said, Voice of All (which presumably has protection from a relevant color) could potentially attack Sonnet safely, and the overall threat assessment of focusing the lower-life player with the more dangerous engine is worth considering. The attack into the open board of Qwen3 is safe but applies less pressure where it matters most.",
      "action_taken": "Attacked Qwen3 Spike (31 life, no flyers) with Kaalia of the Vast",
      "better_line": "Consider attacking Sonnet Vorthos (24 life) with Voice of All if it has protection from blue or black (avoiding Hordewing Skaab blocks), while sending Kaalia at Qwen3 or also at Sonnet if safe. Prioritizing damage on the lower-life player with the more threatening engine could be more impactful.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 158,
      "player": "Gem3P Coach",
      "decision_index": 144,
    },
    {
      "severity": "questionable",
      "description": "Attacking with Paradise Druid unnecessarily sacrifices its hexproof (while untapped) and mana production for just 2 extra damage. Paradise Druid is a key mana source with Imprisoned in the Moon (3 mana) in hand, and keeping it untapped preserves hexproof protection against removal.",
      "action_taken": "Attacked with all creatures including Paradise Druid",
      "better_line": "Attack with only Sram and Puresteel Paladin (4 damage total), keeping Paradise Druid back for mana production and hexproof protection",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 176,
